This packaging targets Ubuntu Resolute (26.04) for ppa:nmccallum/pichi.
The binary package requires dillo-vm from the same archive. Upload remains
on hold until that package is available.

To prepare an unsigned source package from this checkout:

    debian/prepare-source /absolute/path/to/output

Source preparation needs Cargo, Rust, dpkg-dev, patch, GNU tar, and xz.
It may access the network to fetch the dependencies pinned in Cargo.lock.
It works in a temporary copy, copies packaging/host-manifest.patch into
debian/patches/, and applies that host-only manifest patch. The repository
keeps only the shared patch and the Debian series that names its copy.
Preparation runs packaging/trim-cargo-lock to remove unused Cargo.lock
entries and reject any new or changed dependency pin. Preparation then
vendors the dependency graph (copies its sources for offline builds). The output
directory must be outside the source tree and have space for that temporary
copy and its vendor directory; use a disk-backed directory rather than a
size-limited /tmp. Generated dependencies are never committed.

Preparation vendors the shared host dependency graph once. The upstream
(orig) tarball contains the original manifest, the generated trimmed lockfile,
.cargo/vendor.toml and one vendor directory. The lock change is not a patch.
dpkg-source applies the manifest patch from debian/patches using quilt
(Debian's patch series format), so the build sees the matching manifest and
lockfile. This orig tarball includes packaging-generated files; it is not
an unmodified upstream release tarball.
Git dependencies remain pinned to their lockfile revisions and use the same
directory replacements as registry dependencies. The host-only manifest
patch removes artifact dependencies, so neither preparation nor building
needs nightly Cargo or RUSTC_BOOTSTRAP (unlike the Copr preparation command).

The source package builds with the archive's Cargo and Rust, offline and
locked (--frozen). Building a binary from the unpacked source uses:

    dpkg-buildpackage -b -us -uc

Only the host CLI is built. VM integration tests require separately built
guest artifacts and are excluded; the binary's --help is checked instead.
Source preparation and these commands do not upload anything to Launchpad.
